<!-- ============================================================
     Microsoft ADFS Insufficient Access Control EoP Detection Rules
     CVE-2026-56155 (CWE-1220: Insufficient Granularity of Access Control)
     Affected: Microsoft Active Directory Federation Services (ADFS)
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK:
       T1068   - Exploitation for Privilege Escalation (TA0004)
       T1484.002 - Domain Policy Modification: Domain Trust Modification
       T1078.002 - Valid Accounts: Domain Accounts
       T1550.001 - Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token
       T1059.001 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell
     CISA KEV: Added 2026-07-14, Federal Deadline 2026-07-28
     Rule IDs: 103945-103954
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-14

     Vulnerability Summary:

     CVE-2026-56155 - ADFS Insufficient Granularity of Access Control:
       Microsoft Active Directory Federation Services contains a flaw in
       access control granularity (CWE-1220) that allows an authorized
       attacker with existing network access to the ADFS environment to
       elevate privileges locally. The vulnerability affects ADFS relying
       party trust access control enforcement, allowing an attacker to
       obtain tokens for relying parties they are not authorized to
       access, or to modify ADFS configuration to expand their own
       access scope.

       This differs from the DPAPI key extraction technique covered in
       adfs_machine_dpapi_signing_key_extraction (that detection targets
       golden SAML via DPAPI masterkey extraction). CVE-2026-56155 targets
       the ADFS access control evaluation logic directly.

     Detection Approach:

     Section 1 - PowerShell ADFS cmdlet abuse (rules 103945-103949):
       Monitors Sysmon Event 1 (process create) for PowerShell processes
       invoking ADFS administrative cmdlets. The ADFS PowerShell module
       provides commands for modifying Relying Party Trusts, certificate
       configuration, claims provider trusts, and global authentication
       policy. An attacker exploiting CVE-2026-56155 will often use these
       cmdlets to expand access control scope or add a rogue relying party.
       Anchor rule 103945 chains to high-severity child rules (103946-103949)
       that detect specific categories of privileged ADFS modification.

     Section 2 - Windows Security Event lateral movement (rules 103950-103952):
       Windows Security Events 4648 and 4624 capture explicit credential
       use and network logons involving ADFS service accounts. Post-exploit
       lateral movement via CVE-2026-56155 often involves using the
       elevated ADFS token to authenticate as the ADFS service or to
       pivot to additional systems using the acquired privileges.
       Rule 103952 catches the ADFS service host (Microsoft.IdentityServer.
       ServiceHost.exe) spawning unexpected administrative child processes.

     Section 3 - ADFS admin log and scripted-access pattern (rules 103953-103954):
       Rule 103953 monitors the ADFS admin event log channel for Relying
       Party Trust modification events. Rule 103954 fires when the same
       user executes five or more ADFS PowerShell cmdlets within 120
       seconds, indicating scripted enumeration or automated exploitation.

     Log Source Prerequisites:
       Sysmon layer: Sysmon Event 1 (process create) forwarded to Wazuh.
         Fields: win.eventdata.image, win.eventdata.commandLine,
         win.eventdata.user, win.eventdata.parentImage.
       Security log: Windows Security Event Log forwarded to Wazuh.
         Fields: win.system.eventID, win.eventdata.logonType,
         win.eventdata.targetUserName, win.eventdata.ipAddress.
       ADFS admin log: Microsoft-Windows-ADFS/Admin event channel
         forwarded via Windows agent or syslog.

     Severity Tiers:
       103945 (7)   - Anchor: PowerShell with ADFS module reference
       103946 (14)  - High: Relying Party Trust add/modify/remove
       103947 (14)  - High: ADFS certificate or token-signing key change
       103948 (12)  - Medium-High: Claims provider trust modification
       103949 (13)  - High: ADFS global auth policy change
       103950 (13)  - High: Explicit credentials to ADFS service (4648)
       103951 (10)  - Medium: Network logon to ADFS service account (4624)
       103952 (13)  - High: ADFS service host spawns admin tool (4688)
       103953 (12)  - Medium-High: ADFS admin log RPT modification event
       103954 (14)  - High: Rapid ADFS cmdlet burst by same user

     False Positive Guidance:
       103945/103946: Legitimate ADFS administrators may invoke RPT cmdlets
         during planned federation changes; correlate with change ticket
         windows and expected admin accounts.
       103947: Certificate rollover scripts run during scheduled PKI
         maintenance; compare event time against scheduled cert rotation
         tasks before escalating.
       103948: Claims transform policies may be updated during SSO
         onboarding of new applications; baseline known-good operators.
       103950: Service account explicit credential events may appear during
         ADFS proxy or WAP certificate renewal; confirm source host.
       103951: ADFS health checks may produce type-3 logons; baseline
         known monitoring source IPs and suppress if confirmed benign.
       103952: ADFS service host spawning child processes has no benign
         explanation outside of patching scenarios; treat as high-confidence.
       103954: Planned ADFS configuration scripts run by authorized admins
         will trigger this rule; verify operator identity and change ticket.
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="privilege_escalation,adfs,cisa_kev,cve_2026_56155,access_control_bypass,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       SECTION 1: PowerShell-based ADFS administration detection
       Sysmon Event 1 (parent SID 61603)
       Rule IDs: 103945-103949

       The ADFS PowerShell module (ADFS module, loaded via Import-Module ADFS
       or auto-loaded on ADFS servers) exposes cmdlets for every aspect of
       ADFS configuration. Exploitation of CVE-2026-56155 may use these
       cmdlets to modify access control scope (RPTs, claims transforms) or
       to query ADFS state prior to targeted token-forging.

       Rule 103945 is a level-7 anchor that matches any PowerShell or
       pwsh process with ADFS-related string in the command line. Child
       rules 103946-103949 chain from it via if_sid and fire at higher
       severity when specific privileged cmdlet classes are detected.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: PowerShell process with ADFS module or cmdlet reference.
       Fires at level 7 to create a visible correlation event; high-severity
       child rules 103946-103949 chain from this SID when destructive ADFS
       cmdlets are present. Covers both powershell.exe and pwsh.exe (PS7+).
       Matches any commandLine containing recognisable ADFS noun stems so
       that unknown cmdlets from future ADFS module versions are captured. -->
  <rule id="103945" level="7">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Adfs(?:Relying|Claims|Certificate|Properties|Endpoint|Web|Global|Farm|Auth|NativeClient|ServerApp|WebApi)|Import-Module\s+ADFS|Get-Adfs(?:Relying|Claims|Certificate|Properties)|adfs\.psm1)</field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS: PowerShell process $(win.eventdata.image) invoked ADFS module or cmdlet on $(agent.name) by $(win.eventdata.user) -- CVE-2026-56155 access control bypass investigation anchor; child rules will alert on specific privileged operations (T1068/T1484.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1484.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_ps_anchor,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- HIGH: ADFS Relying Party Trust add, modify, or remove.
       Relying Party Trusts define which applications can receive ADFS
       tokens and what claims they receive. Modification of an RPT is the
       most direct path to exploiting CVE-2026-56155: an attacker can
       add a rogue RPT for their controlled application, relax issuance
       authorisation rules, or modify claim transformation to include
       elevated group memberships. Add and Remove operations are equally
       significant; removing a trust can serve as cover for re-adding
       it with weakened access controls. -->
  <rule id="103946" level="14">
    <if_sid>103945</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Add-AdfsRelyingPartyTrust|Set-AdfsRelyingPartyTrust|Remove-AdfsRelyingPartyTrust|Enable-AdfsRelyingPartyTrust|Disable-AdfsRelyingPartyTrust|Update-AdfsRelyingPartyTrust)</field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: Relying Party Trust modified via PowerShell on $(agent.name) by $(win.eventdata.user) [$(win.eventdata.commandLine)] -- unauthorised RPT changes alter token issuance access controls enabling privilege escalation; immediate investigation required (T1484.002/T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1484.002</id>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_rpt_modification,cve_2026_56155,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- HIGH: ADFS certificate or token-signing key modification.
       ADFS uses token-signing certificates to sign issued SAML assertions.
       Modification or rotation of these certificates outside planned PKI
       windows may indicate an attacker preparing to issue forged tokens.
       Set-AdfsCertificate and Update-AdfsCertificate are the primary
       cmdlets; Invoke-AdfsFarmBehaviorLevelRaise changes the farm behaviour
       level and can affect signing algorithm enforcement. -->
  <rule id="103947" level="14">
    <if_sid>103945</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Set-AdfsCertificate|Update-AdfsCertificate|Add-AdfsCertificate|Remove-AdfsCertificate|Set-AdfsAutoGeneratedSigningCertificate|Invoke-AdfsFarmBehaviorLevelRaise)</field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: ADFS certificate or token-signing key modified via PowerShell on $(agent.name) by $(win.eventdata.user) -- signing key changes outside scheduled PKI rotation can enable forged SAML assertion issuance; correlate with change management records (T1068/T1553.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1553.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_cert_modification,cve_2026_56155,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- MEDIUM-HIGH: ADFS claims provider trust or claims transform policy modification.
       Claims provider trusts define external identity sources that ADFS
       trusts. Claims transform policies rewrite incoming claims before
       token issuance. Modification of either can allow an attacker to
       inject arbitrary group membership or role claims into issued tokens,
       achieving effective privilege escalation within all federated
       relying parties. -->
  <rule id="103948" level="12">
    <if_sid>103945</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Add-AdfsClaimsProviderTrust|Set-AdfsClaimsProviderTrust|Remove-AdfsClaimsProviderTrust|Add-AdfsClaimsTransformPolicy|Set-AdfsClaimsTransformPolicy|Remove-AdfsClaimsTransformPolicy|Set-AdfsRelyingPartyClaimsTransformPolicy)</field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: Claims provider trust or claims transform policy modified via PowerShell on $(agent.name) by $(win.eventdata.user) -- claims manipulation allows injection of elevated group memberships into issued tokens (T1484.002/T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1484.002</id>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_claims_modification,cve_2026_56155,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- HIGH: ADFS global authentication policy or endpoint configuration change.
       Set-AdfsProperties controls global ADFS behaviour including token
       lifetime, allowed protocol bindings, and endpoint enablement.
       Set-AdfsGlobalAuthenticationPolicy controls which authentication
       providers are active and their ordering. Set-AdfsEndpoint enables
       or disables individual ADFS protocol endpoints. Changes to any of
       these during CVE-2026-56155 exploitation may weaken access controls
       globally, enable normally-disabled token issuance endpoints, or
       extend token validity to assist in maintaining access after exploit. -->
  <rule id="103949" level="13">
    <if_sid>103945</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Set-AdfsProperties|Set-AdfsGlobalAuthenticationPolicy|Set-AdfsGlobalWebContent|Set-AdfsEndpoint|Enable-AdfsEndpoint|Disable-AdfsEndpoint|Set-AdfsWebConfig|Set-AdfsAdditionalAuthenticationRule)</field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: ADFS global authentication policy or endpoint configuration modified via PowerShell on $(agent.name) by $(win.eventdata.user) -- global ADFS configuration changes can disable access controls or extend token lifetimes to sustain exploit access (T1068/T1484.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1484.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_global_config_modification,cve_2026_56155,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       SECTION 2: Windows Security Event lateral movement detection
       Windows Security Log (parent SID 60103)
       Rule IDs: 103950-103952

       CVE-2026-56155 privilege escalation results in the attacker
       obtaining a token granting elevated access. The next observable
       phase is lateral movement using that elevated token. Windows
       Security Events 4648 (explicit credential logon) and 4624 Type 3
       (network logon) capture authentication events that may reveal the
       ADFS service account or elevated token being used for lateral
       movement. Rule 103952 covers the case where the ADFS service host
       process itself spawns admin tools after successful exploitation.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- HIGH: Explicit credential logon (Event 4648) targeting ADFS-related service.
       Windows Security Event 4648 fires when a process uses explicit
       credentials different from the logged-on user identity. An attacker
       using CVE-2026-56155 to obtain elevated ADFS tokens may use those
       tokens or the underlying ADFS service account to authenticate
       explicitly to downstream services. The targetServerName field in
       4648 events naming ADFS-associated services is a reliable indicator
       of ADFS-related lateral movement. -->
  <rule id="103950" level="13">
    <if_sid>60103</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4648$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetServerName" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:adfs|federation|sts|saml|wsfed|identityserver|stsservice|adfssrv|fedservice)</field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: Explicit credential logon (Event 4648) targeting ADFS service $(win.eventdata.targetServerName) on $(agent.name) from $(win.eventdata.subjectUserName) at $(win.eventdata.ipAddress) -- ADFS service or token used for explicit-credential lateral movement following access control bypass (T1078.002/T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078.002</id>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_lateral_movement,cve_2026_56155,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- MEDIUM: Network logon (Event 4624 Type 3) to ADFS service account.
       Type-3 network logons to the ADFS service account outside of
       expected ADFS health-check or proxy renewal patterns may indicate
       lateral movement using an elevated token obtained via CVE-2026-56155.
       The targetUserName pattern covers common ADFS service account naming
       conventions used in on-premises environments. Tune by adding known
       monitoring system source IPs as suppressions after baselining. -->
  <rule id="103951" level="10">
    <if_sid>60103</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4624$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.logonType">^3$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetUserName" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:adfs|adfssvc|adfs_svc|adfs-svc|adfsservice|adfsfarm|sts_svc|sts-svc|fedsvc|fed_svc|svc_adfs|svc-adfs|federation)</field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: Network logon (Type 3, Event 4624) to ADFS service account $(win.eventdata.targetUserName) on $(agent.name) from $(win.eventdata.ipAddress) -- ADFS service account network authentication; correlate with simultaneous RPT modifications or PowerShell ADFS cmdlet activity (T1078.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_lateral_movement,cve_2026_56155,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- HIGH: ADFS service host spawned an unexpected administrative process (Event 4688).
       Microsoft.IdentityServer.ServiceHost.exe is the ADFS service host
       on Windows Server 2019 and later. It has no legitimate reason to
       spawn command shells, scripting hosts, or administrative utilities
       directly. A child process of this type indicates successful code
       execution within the ADFS service context, which is a strong
       post-exploitation signal for CVE-2026-56155. This rule provides a
       Sysmon-free fallback using Windows process creation audit (4688);
       in Sysmon-enabled environments, rules 103946-103949 will fire first. -->
  <rule id="103952" level="13">
    <if_sid>60103</if_sid>
    <field name="win.system.eventID">^4688$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentProcessName" type="pcre2">(?i)\\Microsoft\.IdentityServer\.ServiceHost\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.newProcessName" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:cmd|powershell|pwsh|wscript|cscript|mshta|net|whoami|nltest|dsquery|csvde|reg|certutil|rundll32)\.exe$</field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: ADFS service host (Microsoft.IdentityServer.ServiceHost.exe) spawned administrative process $(win.eventdata.newProcessName) on $(agent.name) -- ADFS service host should not exec admin tools; confirmed post-exploitation execution in ADFS service context (T1068/T1059.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_unexpected_process,cve_2026_56155,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       SECTION 3: ADFS admin event log and scripted access pattern
       Rule IDs: 103953-103954

       Rule 103953 monitors the ADFS administrative event channel
       (Microsoft-Windows-ADFS/Admin forwarded via the Windows agent)
       for Relying Party Trust modification log entries recorded by
       ADFS itself, independent of the PowerShell cmdlet detection layer.
       This provides defence-in-depth against direct ADFS API calls that
       bypass PowerShell logging.

       Rule 103954 is a frequency rule that fires when five or more events
       matching rule 103945 (ADFS PowerShell anchor) occur within 120 seconds
       from the same user. This pattern is consistent with scripted ADFS
       enumeration or automated exploitation tooling that iterates through
       ADFS cmdlets to map the current access control configuration before
       modifying it.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- MEDIUM-HIGH: ADFS admin event log records Relying Party Trust modification.
       The ADFS administration event channel (Microsoft-Windows-ADFS/Admin)
       records trust configuration changes generated by the ADFS service itself,
       independent of the PowerShell command-line that initiated them. This
       detection layer catches direct ADFS API calls, GUI-based changes via
       the AD FS Management snap-in, or changes made via non-standard tooling
       that would evade the Sysmon process-create detection in rules 103946-103949.
       Match is against common ADFS admin log message patterns for RPT lifecycle
       events. -->
  <rule id="103953" level="12">
    <program_name type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Microsoft-Windows-ADFS|ADFS|AdfsAdmin|AD FS)</program_name>
    <match type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Relying\s+Party\s+Trust|relying_party_trust|RelyingPartyTrust).*?(?:add|creat|modif|updat|chang|remov|delet)</match>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: ADFS admin event log records Relying Party Trust lifecycle change on $(agent.name) -- RPT modification alters token issuance access controls; matches CISA KEV CVE-2026-56155 exploitation pattern; correlate with PowerShell ADFS cmdlet events and operator change records (T1484.002/T1068)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1484.002</id>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_rpt_modification,cve_2026_56155,admin_log,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- HIGH: Rapid burst of ADFS PowerShell cmdlet executions by the same user.
       Automated exploitation of CVE-2026-56155 typically begins with scripted
       enumeration of the existing ADFS access control configuration followed
       by targeted modifications. Five or more ADFS cmdlet invocations within
       120 seconds by the same user is consistent with this pattern. Legitimate
       bulk ADFS configuration changes should be coordinated with change
       management; treat unexpected bursts as high-confidence exploitation
       unless correlated with an approved change ticket. -->
  <rule id="103954" level="14" frequency="5" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>103945</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>win.eventdata.user</same_field>
    <description>Microsoft ADFS CVE-2026-56155 EoP: Rapid ADFS PowerShell cmdlet burst by $(win.eventdata.user) on $(agent.name) -- 5 or more ADFS module invocations within 120 seconds; scripted ADFS enumeration or automated access control bypass exploitation; verify against approved change ticket (T1068/T1484.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1484.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>adfs_scripted_access,cve_2026_56155,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
